msgid "Background {#background}"
msgstr "Background {#background}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This tutorial reproduces the error-detected, approximate preparation of a W state by using dynamic circuits, as presented in [Smith, Pokharel, Maurya, Takita, \"Resilience Beyond the Light Cone: Error-Detected Primitives for Practical Dynamic Circuits\" (2026)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.08925) [\\[1\\]](#references).\n"
msgstr "Ginagaya ng tutorial na ito ang error-detected, approximate na paghahanda ng isang W state gamit ang mga dynamic circuit, tulad ng ipinakita sa [Smith, Pokharel, Maurya, Takita, \"Resilience Beyond the Light Cone: Error-Detected Primitives for Practical Dynamic Circuits\" (2026)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.08925) [\\[1\\]](#references).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The approach is an adaptation of the algorithm presented in [Piroli, Styliaris, Cirac, \"Approximating many-body quantum states with quantum circuits and measurements\" (2024)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.07604) [\\[2\\]](#references), upgraded with the *Distributed Control Error Detection* (DisCo ED) scheme of [Smith et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.08925) The paper runs the protocol on a heavy-hex processor. Here, the protocol runs on the square lattice of a Nighthawk processor. The noisy simulation reproduces its main finding: both DisCo ED checks improve the prepared state compared with no error detection, and the implicit check gives the best state. On hardware at $n = 10$, the idle time during the feedforward limits how much error detection can recover, as discussed after the hardware results.\n"
msgstr "Ang approach ay isang adaptasyon ng algorithm na ipinakita sa [Piroli, Styliaris, Cirac, \"Approximating many-body quantum states with quantum circuits and measurements\" (2024)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.07604) [\\[2\\]](#references), na pinahusay gamit ang *Distributed Control Error Detection* (DisCo ED) scheme ng [Smith et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.08925) Pinapatakbo ng paper ang protocol sa isang heavy-hex processor. Dito, tumatakbo ang protocol sa square lattice ng isang Nighthawk processor. Ginagaya ng noisy simulation ang pangunahing natuklasan nito: pinapabuti ng parehong DisCo ED check ang inihandang state kumpara sa kawalan ng error detection, at ang implicit check ang nagbibigay ng pinakamahusay na state. Sa hardware sa $n = 10$, nililimitahan ng idle time habang may feedforward kung gaano karami ang mababawi ng error detection, gaya ng tinalakay pagkatapos ng mga resulta sa hardware.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The following sections describe the ingredients of this tutorial in detail.\n"
msgstr "Inilalarawan ng mga sumusunod na seksyon nang detalyado ang mga sangkap ng tutorial na ito.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "The W state {#the-w-state}"
msgstr "Ang W state {#the-w-state}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The $n$-qubit W state is the equal superposition of all states with exactly one excitation,\n"
msgstr "Ang $n$-qubit na W state ay ang pantay na superposition ng lahat ng state na may eksaktong isang excitation,\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"|W_n\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\\Big(|10\\cdots0\\rangle + |01\\cdots0\\rangle + \\cdots + |00\\cdots1\\rangle\\Big) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}|e_j\\rangle,\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"|W_n\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\\Big(|10\\cdots0\\rangle + |01\\cdots0\\rangle + \\cdots + |00\\cdots1\\rangle\\Big) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}|e_j\\rangle,\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "where $|e_j\\rangle$ has a single $|1\\rangle$ on qubit $j$. Unlike the GHZ state, its entanglement survives the loss of any one qubit. This tutorial characterizes the prepared state with two simple observables.\n"
msgstr "kung saan ang $|e_j\\rangle$ ay may iisang $|1\\rangle$ sa qubit na $j$. Hindi tulad ng GHZ state, nananatili ang entanglement nito kahit mawala ang alinmang isang qubit. Nililinaw ng tutorial na ito ang inihandang state gamit ang dalawang simpleng observable.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Hamming-weight distribution.** Measure every qubit in the $Z$ basis and count the number of 1s, $w$. A perfect W state gives $P(w=1) = 1$.\n"
msgstr "**Hamming-weight distribution.** Sukatin ang bawat qubit sa $Z$ basis at bilangin ang bilang ng mga 1, $w$. Ang perpektong W state ay nagbibigay ng $P(w=1) = 1$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Coherence.** $P(w=1)=1$ alone does not certify a W state: an incoherent mixture $\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_j |e_j\\rangle\\langle e_j|$, or a superposition $\\sum_j \\pm|e_j\\rangle$ with random signs, also has $P(w=1)=1$. To probe the relative phases, use\n"
msgstr "**Coherence.** Ang $P(w=1)=1$ lamang ay hindi nagpapatunay ng W state: ang incoherent na mixture na $\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_j |e_j\\rangle\\langle e_j|$, o ang superposition na $\\sum_j \\pm|e_j\\rangle$ na may mga random na sign, ay may $P(w=1)=1$ din. Para suriin ang mga relative phase, gamitin ang\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"C = \\frac{n}{4}\\cdot\\frac{1}{n(n-1)}\\sum_{i\\neq j}\\big\\langle X_iX_j + Y_iY_j\\big\\rangle .\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"C = \\frac{n}{4}\\cdot\\frac{1}{n(n-1)}\\sum_{i\\neq j}\\big\\langle X_iX_j + Y_iY_j\\big\\rangle .\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The operator $X_iX_j + Y_iY_j = 2(\\sigma_i^+\\sigma_j^- + \\sigma_i^-\\sigma_j^+)$ moves an excitation between qubits $i$ and $j$. For a state $\\sum_j c_j|e_j\\rangle$ it gives $\\langle X_iX_j + Y_iY_j\\rangle = 4\\,\\mathrm{Re}(c_i^*c_j)$, so $C = 1$ for $|W_n\\rangle$, $C = 0$ for the incoherent mixture, and random signs push $C$ toward zero. Measuring $C$ takes only two extra circuits: one with every W qubit measured in the $X$ basis, and one in the $Y$ basis.\n"
msgstr "Inililipat ng operator na $X_iX_j + Y_iY_j = 2(\\sigma_i^+\\sigma_j^- + \\sigma_i^-\\sigma_j^+)$ ang isang excitation sa pagitan ng mga qubit na $i$ at $j$. Para sa isang state na $\\sum_j c_j|e_j\\rangle$, nagbibigay ito ng $\\langle X_iX_j + Y_iY_j\\rangle = 4\\,\\mathrm{Re}(c_i^*c_j)$, kaya $C = 1$ para sa $|W_n\\rangle$, $C = 0$ para sa incoherent na mixture, at itinutulak ng mga random na sign ang $C$ papunta sa zero. Ang pagsukat ng $C$ ay nangangailangan lamang ng dalawang karagdagang circuit: isa kung saan sinusukat ang bawat W qubit sa $X$ basis, at isa sa $Y$ basis.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Dynamic circuits {#dynamic-circuits}"
msgstr "Mga dynamic circuit {#dynamic-circuits}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "A unitary circuit of nearest-neighbor gates spreads correlations only within a *light cone* that grows by a fixed distance per layer. Preparing a long-range entangled state such as a GHZ or W state on a line of $n$ qubits therefore takes a depth that grows with $n$. Dynamic circuits, which use mid-circuit measurements and classical feedforward, get around this limit: measurement outcomes are processed classically and sent to qubits anywhere on the chip, in a time that does not depend on distance.\n"
msgstr "Ang isang unitary circuit ng mga nearest-neighbor gate ay nagpapakalat ng mga correlation sa loob lamang ng isang *light cone* na lumalaki nang nakapirming distansya bawat layer. Kaya ang paghahanda ng long-range entangled na state tulad ng GHZ o W state sa isang linya ng $n$ qubit ay nangangailangan ng depth na lumalaki kasabay ng $n$. Nalalampasan ng mga dynamic circuit, na gumagamit ng mid-circuit measurement at classical feedforward, ang limitasyong ito: pino-proseso nang classical ang mga resulta ng pagsukat at ipinapadala sa mga qubit saanman sa chip, sa oras na hindi nakadepende sa distansya.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "A paradigmatic example is the constant-depth GHZ state (see the [long-range entanglement tutorial](/tutorials/long-range-entanglement) and [\\[3\\]](#references)). The protocol splits the line into small *patches* and prepares small GHZ states on each patch in parallel. It then *fuses* neighboring patches by measuring the parity $Z_aZ_b$ across each boundary. Outcome $m_k = 1$ means the two patches disagree. Applying $X$ to every qubit of patch $k$ whenever the cumulative parity $m_1 \\oplus \\cdots \\oplus m_k$ is 1 fixes every disagreement in a single round of feedforward. In this circuit, each measured qubit is then reset and re-entangled with its own patch, so all $n$ qubits end up in\n"
msgstr "Isang huwarang halimbawa ay ang constant-depth na GHZ state (tingnan ang [long-range entanglement tutorial](/tutorials/long-range-entanglement) at [\\[3\\]](#references)). Hinahati ng protocol ang linya sa maliliit na *patch* at sabay-sabay na naghahanda ng maliliit na GHZ state sa bawat patch. Pagkatapos ay *pinagsasama* nito ang magkakalapit na patch sa pamamagitan ng pagsukat ng parity na $Z_aZ_b$ sa bawat hangganan. Ang resultang $m_k = 1$ ay nangangahulugang hindi nagtutugma ang dalawang patch. Ang paglalapat ng $X$ sa bawat qubit ng patch $k$ tuwing ang cumulative parity na $m_1 \\oplus \\cdots \\oplus m_k$ ay 1 ay nag-aayos sa bawat hindi pagkakatugma sa isang round ng feedforward. Sa circuit na ito, ang bawat sinukat na qubit ay ni-reset at muling ine-entangle sa sarili nitong patch, kaya ang lahat ng $n$ qubit ay mapupunta sa\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"|\\mathrm{GHZ}\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\big(|0\\cdots0\\rangle + |1\\cdots1\\rangle\\big),\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"|\\mathrm{GHZ}\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\big(|0\\cdots0\\rangle + |1\\cdots1\\rangle\\big),\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "in a depth that does not depend on $n$.\n"
msgstr "sa depth na hindi nakadepende sa $n$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Approximate preparation of the W state {#approximate-preparation-of-the-w-state}"
msgstr "Approximate na paghahanda ng W state {#approximate-preparation-of-the-w-state}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The idea of the W-state-preparation protocol of Ref. [\\[2\\]](#references) is to start from a product state that overlaps strongly with $|W\\rangle$, then *project* away the unwanted components. Rotating every qubit with $R_Y(\\theta)$, where $\\sin^2(\\theta/2) = p$, gives\n"
msgstr "Ang ideya ng W-state-preparation protocol ng Ref. [\\[2\\]](#references) ay magsimula sa isang product state na lubos na nag-o-overlap sa $|W\\rangle$, pagkatapos ay *i-project* palayo ang mga hindi gustong component. Ang pag-rotate sa bawat qubit gamit ang $R_Y(\\theta)$, kung saan $\\sin^2(\\theta/2) = p$, ay nagbibigay ng\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"|\\phi\\rangle = \\bigotimes_{j}\\Big(\\sqrt{1-p}\\,|0\\rangle + \\sqrt{p}\\,|1\\rangle\\Big) = \\sum_{k=0}^{n}\\sqrt{P_k}\\,|D_k\\rangle, \\qquad P_k = \\binom{n}{k}p^k(1-p)^{n-k},\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"|\\phi\\rangle = \\bigotimes_{j}\\Big(\\sqrt{1-p}\\,|0\\rangle + \\sqrt{p}\\,|1\\rangle\\Big) = \\sum_{k=0}^{n}\\sqrt{P_k}\\,|D_k\\rangle, \\qquad P_k = \\binom{n}{k}p^k(1-p)^{n-k},\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "where $|D_k\\rangle$ is the uniform superposition of all weight-$k$ states (the Dicke state, with $|D_1\\rangle = |W\\rangle$). For small $p$, almost all of the weight is in $k=0$ and $k=1$. Projecting onto odd parity with $\\Pi_{\\rm odd} = (1 - Z^{\\otimes n})/2$ removes $k = 0, 2, 4, \\ldots$ and leaves\n"
msgstr "kung saan ang $|D_k\\rangle$ ay ang uniform superposition ng lahat ng weight-$k$ na state (ang Dicke state, na may $|D_1\\rangle = |W\\rangle$). Para sa maliit na $p$, halos lahat ng weight ay nasa $k=0$ at $k=1$. Ang pag-project sa odd parity gamit ang $\\Pi_{\\rm odd} = (1 - Z^{\\otimes n})/2$ ay nag-aalis sa $k = 0, 2, 4, \\ldots$ at nag-iiwan ng\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\Pi_{\\rm odd}|\\phi\\rangle \\propto \\sqrt{P_1}\\,|W\\rangle + \\sqrt{P_3}\\,|D_3\\rangle + \\cdots .\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\Pi_{\\rm odd}|\\phi\\rangle \\propto \\sqrt{P_1}\\,|W\\rangle + \\sqrt{P_3}\\,|D_3\\rangle + \\cdots .\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The projection succeeds with probability $r_s = \\sum_{k\\,\\rm odd}P_k = \\big[1 - (1-2p)^n\\big]/2$, and the fidelity with the W state is $F = P_1/r_s$. Choosing $p = \\delta/n$, for large $n$\n"
msgstr "Nagtatagumpay ang projection na may probabilidad na $r_s = \\sum_{k\\,\\rm odd}P_k = \\big[1 - (1-2p)^n\\big]/2$, at ang fidelity sa W state ay $F = P_1/r_s$. Sa pagpili ng $p = \\delta/n$, para sa malaking $n$\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"F \\to \\frac{\\delta}{\\sinh\\delta} \\approx 1 - \\frac{\\delta^2}{6}, \\qquad r_s \\to \\frac{1 - e^{-2\\delta}}{2}.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"F \\to \\frac{\\delta}{\\sinh\\delta} \\approx 1 - \\frac{\\delta^2}{6}, \\qquad r_s \\to \\frac{1 - e^{-2\\delta}}{2}.\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Neither depends on $n$, so the cost of the protocol does not grow with system size. The parameter $\\delta$ trades fidelity against success rate. This tutorial uses $\\delta = 0.3$, which gives $F \\approx 0.985$ and $r_s \\approx 0.23$. The small remaining infidelity comes from higher odd-weight components.\n"
msgstr "Wala sa dalawa ang nakadepende sa $n$, kaya hindi lumalaki ang gastos ng protocol kasabay ng laki ng sistema. Ang parameter na $\\delta$ ang nagbabalanse sa fidelity laban sa success rate. Ginagamit ng tutorial na ito ang $\\delta = 0.3$, na nagbibigay ng $F \\approx 0.985$ at $r_s \\approx 0.23$. Ang maliit na natitirang infidelity ay nagmumula sa mas matataas na odd-weight na component.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Single-ancilla projection.** One way to apply $\\Pi_{\\rm odd}$ is a Hadamard test. Prepare an ancilla in $|+\\rangle$, apply $CZ$ between the ancilla and every system qubit (a controlled-$Z^{\\otimes n}$), and measure the ancilla in the $X$ basis:\n"
msgstr "**Single-ancilla projection.** Isang paraan para ilapat ang $\\Pi_{\\rm odd}$ ay ang Hadamard test. Maghanda ng ancilla sa $|+\\rangle$, ilapat ang $CZ$ sa pagitan ng ancilla at ng bawat system qubit (isang controlled-$Z^{\\otimes n}$), at sukatin ang ancilla sa $X$ basis:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\Big(|0\\rangle|\\phi\\rangle + |1\\rangle Z^{\\otimes n}|\\phi\\rangle\\Big) = |+\\rangle\\,\\frac{1 + Z^{\\otimes n}}{2}|\\phi\\rangle + |-\\rangle\\,\\frac{1 - Z^{\\otimes n}}{2}|\\phi\\rangle .\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\Big(|0\\rangle|\\phi\\rangle + |1\\rangle Z^{\\otimes n}|\\phi\\rangle\\Big) = |+\\rangle\\,\\frac{1 + Z^{\\otimes n}}{2}|\\phi\\rangle + |-\\rangle\\,\\frac{1 - Z^{\\otimes n}}{2}|\\phi\\rangle .\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Outcome \"$-$\" heralds the odd sector. The catch is that a single ancilla must interact with all $n$ qubits, requiring a circuit depth proportional to $n$.\n"
msgstr "Ang resultang \"$-$\" ay nagpapahiwatig ng odd sector. Ang problema ay kailangang makipag-interact ng iisang ancilla sa lahat ng $n$ qubit, na nangangailangan ng circuit depth na proporsyonal sa $n$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Distributed control.** Replace the single ancilla with an $n$-qubit GHZ state prepared via dynamic circuits, and place the GHZ qubit $g_j$ next to W qubit $w_j$. The GHZ state behaves as one *logical* qubit, with $|\\bar 0\\rangle = |0\\cdots0\\rangle$, $|\\bar 1\\rangle = |1\\cdots1\\rangle$, and logical operator $\\bar X = X^{\\otimes n}$. The controlled-$Z^{\\otimes n}$ becomes $CZ(g_j, w_j)$ on every pair at once, a single layer of gates. Measuring $\\bar X$ means measuring every GHZ qubit in the $X$ basis and multiplying the outcomes, so an odd number of 1s heralds success. Every step now has constant depth. On a square lattice, the GHZ line and the W line form a *ladder*: two rows of $n$ qubits joined by $n$ rungs.\n"
msgstr "**Distributed control.** Palitan ang iisang ancilla ng $n$-qubit na GHZ state na inihanda gamit ang mga dynamic circuit, at ilagay ang GHZ qubit na $g_j$ sa tabi ng W qubit na $w_j$. Kumikilos ang GHZ state bilang isang *logical* na qubit, na may $|\\bar 0\\rangle = |0\\cdots0\\rangle$, $|\\bar 1\\rangle = |1\\cdots1\\rangle$, at logical operator na $\\bar X = X^{\\otimes n}$. Ang controlled-$Z^{\\otimes n}$ ay nagiging $CZ(g_j, w_j)$ sa bawat pares nang sabay-sabay, isang layer ng mga gate. Ang pagsukat ng $\\bar X$ ay nangangahulugang pagsukat ng bawat GHZ qubit sa $X$ basis at pagmu-multiply ng mga resulta, kaya ang odd na bilang ng mga 1 ay nagpapahiwatig ng tagumpay. Ngayon, constant depth na ang bawat step. Sa isang square lattice, ang GHZ line at ang W line ay bumubuo ng isang *ladder*: dalawang hilera ng $n$ qubit na pinagdugtong ng $n$ rung.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Ladder layout of the GHZ line and the W line on the square lattice](/docs/images/tutorials/error-detected-w-state/w-state-ladder.avif) *(a) The GHZ line and the W line occupy two neighboring rows of the square lattice and are joined by $CZ(g_j, w_j)$ rungs. (b) The GHZ line alone. The staggered prep patches (shaded) are fused by mid-circuit measurements at the marked boundaries. Each boundary falls inside one of the DisCo ED detection pairs (check $c$, root $r$), so a faulty fusion is detected.*\n"
msgstr "![Ladder layout of the GHZ line and the W line on the square lattice](/docs/images/tutorials/error-detected-w-state/w-state-ladder.avif) *(a) The GHZ line and the W line occupy two neighboring rows of the square lattice and are joined by $CZ(g_j, w_j)$ rungs. (b) The GHZ line alone. The staggered prep patches (shaded) are fused by mid-circuit measurements at the marked boundaries. Each boundary falls inside one of the DisCo ED detection pairs (check $c$, root $r$), so a faulty fusion is detected.*\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "DisCo ED {#disco-ed}"
msgstr "DisCo ED {#disco-ed}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "While the distributed control technique reduces the procedure to a constant depth circuit, it does not come for free.  It requires $n$ ancillas and, more importantly, $\\lceil n/2 \\rceil - 1$ mid-circuit measurements. Readout error is typically slightly worse than two-qubit gate error on superconducting devices, so implementing this strategy can introduce significant noise. Additional challenges include the fact that the GHZ qubits sit idle during the mid-circuit measurements and feedforward, making them susceptible to relaxation and dephasing errors, and the final $\\bar X$ readout requires measurement of an $n$-qubit operator.\n"
msgstr "Bagama't binabawasan ng distributed control technique ang procedure sa isang constant depth na circuit, hindi ito libre. Nangangailangan ito ng $n$ ancilla at, higit sa lahat, $\\lceil n/2 \\rceil - 1$ mid-circuit measurement. Karaniwang bahagyang mas malala ang readout error kaysa sa two-qubit gate error sa mga superconducting device, kaya ang pag-implement ng estratehiyang ito ay maaaring magpasok ng malaking ingay. Kasama sa karagdagang hamon ang katotohanang walang ginagawa ang mga GHZ qubit sa panahon ng mga mid-circuit measurement at feedforward, kaya madali silang tamaan ng relaxation at dephasing error, at ang huling $\\bar X$ readout ay nangangailangan ng pagsukat ng isang $n$-qubit na operator.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "To dig further into the impact of noise, consider the effect of each type of error on the GHZ state throughout this protocol:\n"
msgstr "Para mas maunawaan ang epekto ng ingay, isaalang-alang ang epekto ng bawat uri ng error sa GHZ state sa buong protocol na ito:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Bit flips:** The CZ layer turns $X_{g_j}$ (with $g_j$ denoting the $j$-th GHZ qubit) into $X_{g_j}Z_{w_j}$ (with $w_j$ denoting the $j$-th W state qubit), so a bit flip on $g_j$ applies $Z$ to $w_j$. Because $X_{g_j}$ commutes with $\\bar X$, the herald is unaffected, and the error flips the sign of one W amplitude. The Hamming weight is blind to this, but $C$ drops.\n"
msgstr "**Mga bit flip:** Ginagawa ng CZ layer ang $X_{g_j}$ (kung saan ang $g_j$ ay tumutukoy sa $j$-th na GHZ qubit) na $X_{g_j}Z_{w_j}$ (kung saan ang $w_j$ ay tumutukoy sa $j$-th na W state qubit), kaya ang bit flip sa $g_j$ ay naglalapat ng $Z$ sa $w_j$. Dahil nagko-commute ang $X_{g_j}$ sa $\\bar X$, hindi naaapektuhan ang herald, at binabaligtad ng error ang sign ng isang W amplitude. Hindi ito nakikita ng Hamming weight, ngunit bumababa ang $C$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Fusion readout errors:** Because the parity of fusion mid-circuit measurement outcomes determines the pattern of X-corrections applied via feedforward when preparing the GHZ state, a wrong fusion outcome creates a pattern of bit flip errors called a *domain wall*. Depending on how the feedforward corrections are applied, a domain wall between sites $j$ and $j+1$ produces a bit-flip error on all GHZ qubits to the left (or right) of the domain wall. The impact of these bit flip errors on the state is as discussed above.\n"
msgstr "**Mga fusion readout error:** Dahil ang parity ng mga resulta ng fusion mid-circuit measurement ang tumutukoy sa pattern ng mga X-correction na inilalapat sa pamamagitan ng feedforward kapag inihahanda ang GHZ state, ang maling fusion outcome ay lumilikha ng pattern ng mga bit flip error na tinatawag na *domain wall*. Depende sa kung paano inilalapat ang mga feedforward correction, ang domain wall sa pagitan ng mga site na $j$ at $j+1$ ay gumagawa ng bit-flip error sa lahat ng GHZ qubit sa kaliwa (o kanan) ng domain wall. Ang epekto ng mga bit flip error na ito sa state ay gaya ng tinalakay sa itaas.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Phase flips and readout errors** on the GHZ qubits flip the herald, so the protocol accepts the even-parity sector by mistake. These show up as weight 0 and weight 2 in the Hamming-weight distribution.\n"
msgstr "**Mga phase flip at readout error** sa mga GHZ qubit ay bumabaligtad sa herald, kaya mali ang pagtanggap ng protocol sa even-parity na sector. Lumilitaw ang mga ito bilang weight 0 at weight 2 sa Hamming-weight distribution.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "DisCo ED starts from the observation that the GHZ state is a repetition code: its stabilizers are $Z_jZ_{j+1}$ and its logical operator is $\\bar X$. A plain readout measures each qubit in the $X$ basis, which reveals $\\bar X$ but by gathering independent pieces of information that are not useful to the protocol (that is, each individual $X$). The idea of DisCo ED is to replace these \"useless\" measurements with more useful ones, while still gathering the necessary information to construct $\\bar X$. In particular, group the GHZ qubits into pairs $(2k, 2k+1)$, each with a *check* qubit $c = 2k$ and a *root* qubit $r = 2k+1$. Because $X_cX_r$ and $Z_cZ_r$ commute, you can measure both. A $CX$ from $r$ to $c$ maps $X_cX_r \\to X_r$ and $Z_cZ_r \\to Z_c$, so afterward:\n"
msgstr "Nagsisimula ang DisCo ED sa obserbasyon na ang GHZ state ay isang repetition code: ang mga stabilizer nito ay $Z_jZ_{j+1}$ at ang logical operator nito ay $\\bar X$. Ang payak na readout ay sumusukat sa bawat qubit sa $X$ basis, na naglalantad ng $\\bar X$ ngunit sa pamamagitan ng pangangalap ng mga independiyenteng piraso ng impormasyon na hindi kapaki-pakinabang sa protocol (ibig sabihin, bawat indibidwal na $X$). Ang ideya ng DisCo ED ay palitan ang mga \"walang silbing\" pagsukat na ito ng mas kapaki-pakinabang, habang nangangalap pa rin ng kinakailangang impormasyon para buuin ang $\\bar X$. Sa partikular, pagpangkatin ang mga GHZ qubit sa mga pares na $(2k, 2k+1)$, bawat isa ay may *check* qubit na $c = 2k$ at *root* qubit na $r = 2k+1$. Dahil nagko-commute ang $X_cX_r$ at $Z_cZ_r$, maaari mong sukatin ang pareho. Ang $CX$ mula $r$ papuntang $c$ ay nagma-map ng $X_cX_r \\to X_r$ at $Z_cZ_r \\to Z_c$, kaya pagkatapos nito:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "the root, measured in the $X$ basis, gives bit $r_k$, the value of $X_cX_r$. The product of all root outcomes is $\\bar X$, so the herald is an odd number of 1s among the $r_k$.\n"
msgstr "ang root, na sinusukat sa $X$ basis, ay nagbibigay ng bit na $r_k$, ang halaga ng $X_cX_r$. Ang product ng lahat ng root outcome ay $\\bar X$, kaya ang herald ay isang odd na bilang ng mga 1 sa mga $r_k$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "the check, measured in the $Z$ basis, gives bit $f_k$, the value of the stabilizer $Z_cZ_r$. Without errors, $f_k = 0$.\n"
msgstr "ang check, na sinusukat sa $Z$ basis, ay nagbibigay ng bit na $f_k$, ang halaga ng stabilizer na $Z_cZ_r$. Kung walang error, $f_k = 0$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This gives two variants.\n"
msgstr "Nagbibigay ito ng dalawang variant.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Explicit check:** $CX(r\\to c)$, then $H$ on $r$. Reject the shot if any $f_k = 1$.\n"
msgstr "**Explicit check:** $CX(r\\to c)$, pagkatapos ay $H$ sa $r$. Tanggihan ang shot kung may anumang $f_k = 1$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Implicit check:** add a second $CX(r\\to c)$ after the $H$. The root still gives $X_cX_r$, but the check now measures the product $X_cX_r \\cdot Z_cZ_r$, so without errors $f_k = r_k$. Reject the shot if any $f_k \\ne r_k$.\n"
msgstr "**Implicit check:** magdagdag ng pangalawang $CX(r\\to c)$ pagkatapos ng $H$. Ibinibigay pa rin ng root ang $X_cX_r$, ngunit sinusukat na ngayon ng check ang product na $X_cX_r \\cdot Z_cZ_r$, kaya kung walang error, $f_k = r_k$. Tanggihan ang shot kung may anumang $f_k \\ne r_k$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Both variants check the same stabilizers. The difference is what happens when a readout error occurs on a root qubit: it flips the herald without touching any stabilizer, so the explicit check misses it, while the implicit check catches it because $f_k$ no longer matches $r_k$. The implicit readout $CX\\cdot H\\cdot CX$ compiles to the same single two-qubit gate as $CX\\cdot H$, so this extra protection costs no additional two-qubit gates.\n"
msgstr "Sinusuri ng dalawang variant ang parehong mga stabilizer. Ang pagkakaiba ay kung ano ang nangyayari kapag may readout error sa isang root qubit: binabaligtad nito ang herald nang hindi ginagalaw ang anumang stabilizer, kaya hindi ito nahuhuli ng explicit check, samantalang nahuhuli ito ng implicit check dahil hindi na tumutugma ang $f_k$ sa $r_k$. Ang implicit readout na $CX\\cdot H\\cdot CX$ ay nako-compile sa parehong iisang two-qubit gate gaya ng $CX\\cdot H$, kaya ang karagdagang proteksyong ito ay hindi nangangailangan ng dagdag na two-qubit gate.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Each pair is a detection patch of size $d = 2$. The paper also considers larger patches, which check more stabilizers at the cost of a deeper readout; this tutorial uses $d = 2$ throughout. With $d=2$, DisCo ED detects:\n"
msgstr "Ang bawat pares ay isang detection patch na may sukat na $d = 2$. Isinasaalang-alang din ng paper ang mas malalaking patch, na sumusuri ng mas maraming stabilizer kapalit ng mas malalim na readout; ginagamit ng tutorial na ito ang $d = 2$ sa kabuuan. Sa $d=2$, natutukoy ng DisCo ED ang:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**any single bit flip on a GHZ qubit**, because every GHZ qubit belongs to exactly one checked pair.\n"
msgstr "**anumang iisang bit flip sa isang GHZ qubit**, dahil ang bawat GHZ qubit ay kabilang sa eksaktong isang naka-check na pares.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**fusion errors**, provided the domain wall falls *inside* a pair. This is why the GHZ preparation, as shown below, uses the staggered patches $[0], [1,2], [3,4], \\ldots$: their boundaries $(0|1), (2|3), \\ldots$ lie inside the detection pairs $[0,1], [2,3], \\ldots$. If the prep patches were aligned with the pairs, every domain wall would fall *between* two pairs, where nothing is checked.\n"
msgstr "**mga fusion error**, basta ang domain wall ay bumagsak *sa loob* ng isang pares. Ito ang dahilan kung bakit gumagamit ang GHZ preparation, gaya ng ipinapakita sa ibaba, ng mga staggered na patch na $[0], [1,2], [3,4], \\ldots$: ang mga hangganan nilang $(0|1), (2|3), \\ldots$ ay nasa loob ng mga detection pair na $[0,1], [2,3], \\ldots$. Kung ang mga prep patch ay nakahanay sa mga pares, bawat domain wall ay babagsak *sa pagitan* ng dalawang pares, kung saan walang sinusuri.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**readout errors on the root qubits**, with the implicit check only.\n"
msgstr "**mga readout error sa mga root qubit**, sa implicit check lamang.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-20
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "DisCo ED does not detect phase flips on the GHZ qubits, or any error on the W qubits themselves, so postselection improves the state without making it perfect. The price is the fraction of shots discarded (the *acceptance rate* is less than 1) and one extra layer of two-qubit gates in the readout. No additional qubits are needed over the non-error detected implementation.\n"
msgstr "Hindi natutukoy ng DisCo ED ang mga phase flip sa mga GHZ qubit, o anumang error sa mga W qubit mismo, kaya pinapabuti ng postselection ang state nang hindi ito ginagawang perpekto. Ang kapalit ay ang bahagi ng mga shot na itinatapon (ang *acceptance rate* ay mas mababa sa 1) at isang karagdagang layer ng mga two-qubit gate sa readout. Hindi na kailangan ng karagdagang qubit kumpara sa implementasyong walang error detection.\n"

